implementations.code:
//! The `bsc shot` subcommand (#3261, epic #3260) — ask the RUNNING app for a webview snapshot and print
//! the PNG path, so an external session can SEE the app rather than infer what it looks like.
//!
//! Dispatched by the unified `bsc` binary (#1877) via [`run`]; the shared per-command help (#1762):
//!   bsc shot help          # compact menu
//!   bsc shot take help     # detailed help for ONE command
//!
//! Two directories, deliberately: the CHANNEL ([`bsc_appchan::chan_dir`], shared with `bsc navigate`)
//! carries the request/reply files; `~/.base-studio-code/shots/` (or `BSC_SHOT_DIR`) holds the PNGs the
//! caller owns. Sweeping the transport must never be able to delete someone's screenshots.

use crate::{is_png, shots_dir, Rect, ShotRequest, ShotResult, KIND};
use bsc_cli_util::CmdDoc;

const TAGLINE: &str = "capture the RUNNING app's real pixels — a webview snapshot (#3261)";

/// How long a `take` waits for the app. The app answers in well under a second when it is running; this
/// bound exists so a request made with NO app running fails fast and clearly instead of hanging a loop
/// iteration forever.
const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS: i64 = 8_000;
const POLL_INTERVAL_MS: u64 = 40;

/// Requests older than this are swept on each `take` — an abandoned CLI (Ctrl-C'd mid-poll) or one made
/// while no app was running must not accumulate, nor be served later to nobody.
const STALE_MS: i64 = 60_000;

/// Default gap between burst frames (`--frames`). Each frame ALSO round-trips the channel, so the real
/// gap is `max(this, the round-trip)` — a few frames across an animation, not a high-fps video.
const DEFAULT_INTERVAL_MS: i64 = 120;
/// Runaway guard on `--frames` — a burst is "a few frames across an animation", not a screen recording.
const MAX_FRAMES: i64 = 120;

/// The crate's own verb catalog. `pub` so `bsc surface verbs` and the Glance crate drill can PROJECT
/// it rather than restate it — a generated catalog cannot drift, a second list does.
pub const COMMANDS: &[CmdDoc] = &[
    CmdDoc {
        name: "take",
        summary: "capture the app's webview; prints the PNG path",
        usage: "\
USAGE:
  bsc shot take [--rect x,y,w,h] [--window <label>] [--out <path>] [--frames <N>] [--interval <ms>] [--timeout <ms>] [--json|--pretty]

Asks the RUNNING desktop app to snapshot its webview and writes a PNG. Prints the path (--json emits
{ path, w, h }; a burst emits { frames: [ … ] }).

  --rect x,y,w,h   crop to a region, in CSS pixels from the webview's top-left. Omit for the whole
                   webview. The frontend knows a preview iframe's getBoundingClientRect(), so
                   'just the component' is a crop, not a second capture path.
  --window <label> WHICH webview to capture. Default `main`. The app is not one window: a widget is a
                   separate webview labelled `bsc-widget-<id>`, and until this flag existed every
                   capture resolved `main` unconditionally -- so the tear-off surfaces, the ones
                   nothing else can see, were exactly the ones this tool could not photograph. A label
                   that names no window is refused with the list of labels that DO exist, so finding
                   one needs no second command.
  --out <path>     where to write the PNG. Default: <shots dir>/<id>.png. With --frames, this is the
                   BASE: <base>-01.<ext>, <base>-02.<ext>, …
  --frames <N>     capture a BURST of N frames (default 1) — for ANIMATIONS, where one frame can't show
                   motion. Prints N paths, oldest first.
  --interval <ms>  gap between burst frames (default 120). See TIMING below.
  --timeout <ms>   how long to wait for the app, per frame (default 8000).

WHAT IT CAPTURES
  The webview's COMPOSITED output — pixel-identical to what you see, including the sandboxed preview
  iframe, and a render rather than a screen grab. Verified: a capture taken while the window was
  MINIMIZED is byte-identical to a visible one, so the overnight case (#3260) cannot silently produce
  black frames.

BURST CAPTURE (--frames)
  Frames land as a numbered set — <base>-01.png, <base>-02.png, … from --out if given, else
  <shots>/<id>-NN.png sharing one burst id. If the app stops mid-burst, the frames already captured
  STAY on disk and the error says how many landed (a partial burst is still useful).

TIMING
  Each frame round-trips the file channel (write request → the app's watcher → snapshot → reply), so
  the REAL gap between frames is max(--interval, that round-trip). High fps isn't reachable this way,
  but 'a few frames across an animation' is — enough to GROUND a motion change, not to record video.

REQUIRES THE APP RUNNING
  `bsc` cannot call the app (the bridge only runs app→bsc), so this drops a request in the channel dir
  and waits for the app's watcher. No app ⇒ a clear timeout error, never a hang.

PAIR IT WITH NAVIGATE
  A shot captures whatever is on screen. To target something, steer first:
    bsc navigate component <kit> <component> && bsc shot take",
    },
    CmdDoc {
        name: "preview",
        summary: "capture JUST the Design Studio's component preview (auto-cropped)",
        usage: "\
USAGE:
  bsc shot preview [--frames <N>] [--interval <ms>] [--out <path>] [--timeout <ms>] [--json|--pretty]

Captures JUST the component preview — the app resolves the Design Studio's preview element's rect
ITSELF and crops to it, so you don't pass coordinates. This is the DESIGNER's ground truth: the
component, not the whole app chrome.

  --frames <N>     a BURST of N frames (for animations) — same as `take` (see `bsc shot take help`).
  --interval <ms>  gap between burst frames (default 120).
  --out <path>     where to write the PNG (base for a burst). Default <shots>/<id>.png.
  --timeout <ms>   how long to wait for the app, per frame (default 8000).

STEER FIRST
  The preview shows the SELECTED component, so land one first:
    bsc navigate component <kit> <component> && bsc shot preview

NO COMPONENT SHOWN
  If the Design Studio isn't showing a component (no preview element registered), this errors clearly
  rather than capturing the whole screen.

  If `bsc navigate component` did not bring it on screen, the Studio is not the visible page and the
  navigate DECLINED ON PURPOSE (#3599): a background follow-along does not steal the user's view. Do NOT
  force it with `navigate page` / `--workspace` while someone is working — that is the yank #4248 is
  about. Capture when the Studio is showing, or leave the frame for the unattended loop.",
    },
    CmdDoc {
        name: "analyze",
        summary: "capture, analyse and REAP — the verdict outlives the pixels",
        usage: "\
USAGE:
  bsc shot analyze [<component>] [--rect x,y,w,h] [--window <label>] [--engine <e>] [--keep] [--timeout <ms>] [--json|--pretty]
  bsc shot analyze --file <path.png> [--engine <e>] [--json|--pretty]

Captures, reads the pixels, prints what it found, and DELETES the PNG. With no positional it analyses the
whole webview; with a component id it analyses that component's mounted preview, as `frame` does.

  --file <path>    read a PNG that ALREADY EXISTS instead of capturing one. NEVER reaped, and not
                   subject to --keep: the reap is only ever justified because the capture was ours and
                   transient, and deleting a file you handed us would be a destructive surprise from a
                   verb whose job is reading. It is also how the engine is exercised with no app
                   running — `diff` has always read files; this closes that asymmetry.

The point is that nothing accumulates: the capture exists for the length of this command, so the shots
dir's retention window never applies to it. What survives is the VERDICT, which is what you wanted from
the image anyway.

  --window <label> which webview to read (default `main`) — the only way to answer DID ANYTHING RENDER
                   about a widget, which is a separate window
  --engine <e>     which analyser reads the pixels (default: pixels)
  --keep           do not reap; print where the PNG landed instead (a capture only)
  --timeout <ms>   how long to wait for the app (default 8000)

ENGINES:
  pixels           a local census: blank · colours · background · ink · empty tiles. No model, no
                   network, no reference render needed.

WHAT `pixels` ANSWERS, and what it does not. It answers DID ANYTHING RENDER — the question `diff` cannot
reach, because a blank frame compares clean against another blank frame and reports `unchanged` about a
component that has been broken the whole time. It does NOT answer whether the design is any good.

Everything reported is a MEASUREMENT except `blank`, which is a definition (one colour, after
quantization) rather than a tuned threshold. `ink` and `emptyTiles` are given so you can apply a floor
you have actually measured for your own subject — a constant chosen here would be a guess wearing a
number's clothes.

A FAILED analysis still reaps. A capture must not survive because the thing that was going to read it
errored — that is exactly where leftovers pile up unseen. A capture under <shots>/baselines/ is never
reaped, however it got named: baselines are the one thing here that is not regenerable on demand.",
    },
    CmdDoc {
        name: "diff",
        summary: "compare two captures — how much changed, and WHERE (scalars only)",
        usage: "USAGE:
  bsc shot diff <baseline.png> <current.png> [--tolerance <f>] [--json|--pretty]
  bsc shot diff <component>                  [--tolerance <f>]   # against its saved baseline

Reports how far `current` moved from `baseline`. NO IMAGE DATA is produced or returned — the answer is
numbers, so an agent can act on a render it never has to look at:

  distance         mean absolute RGB difference over the frame, 0..1
  worstTile        the highest per-tile difference on an 8x8 grid, same scale
  tile             [col,row] of that tile — WHERE it moved
  changedFraction  fraction of pixels differing at all, 0..1

READ `worstTile`, NOT `changedFraction`. Measured on two captures of an unchanged component: 2.6% of
pixels differed at an amplitude of 0.33/255. Anti-aliasing and subpixel text move a LOT of pixels by a
tiny amount, so a pixel COUNT — and a hash, which is stricter still — reports `changed` on every run. A
real change is CONCENTRATED, which is what the tile grid measures.

CALIBRATE, do not guess. Capture the same subject twice with nothing changed and use that `distance` as
the subject's noise floor; `--tolerance` then decides same/changed against a number you measured. Without
it the command only reports.

Different dimensions are their own answer (`mismatch: dimensions`), never rescaled — a resized render
means the layout moved, which is the strongest evidence available.",
    },
    CmdDoc {
        name: "baseline",
        summary: "save a component's NAMED reference render — the thing `diff <component>` compares against",
        usage: "USAGE:
  bsc shot baseline <component> [--timeout <ms>] [--json|--pretty]
  bsc shot baseline --kit <kit>  [--timeout <ms>] [--json|--pretty]   # sweep the whole kit
  bsc shot baseline list

Captures the mounted preview of <component> and keeps it as that component's reference, under
<shots>/baselines/<component>.png. Same subject resolution and same NOT-MOUNTED error as `frame` —
only the destination differs, and it is not yours to choose (no --out).

WHY A NAMED ONE. `diff` already tells you how far two captures moved. What it could not do is know
WHICH two you meant: a before/after check meant capturing to one path, remembering which was the
'before', capturing again, and passing both. Across a 45-component refactor that is 45 chances to
compare the wrong pair — and a wrong pair fails SILENTLY, reporting a plausible number about two
images nobody intended to compare.

  bsc navigate component <kit> <id>   # land it
  bsc shot baseline <id>              # save the reference
  … change the component …
  bsc shot frame <id>                 # capture the current render
  bsc shot diff <id>                  # compare the two, by name

`baseline list` prints which components have a reference saved.

SWEEPING A KIT (--kit). Captures a reference for EVERY component in the kit, landing each one itself
so you do not run 45 navigate+shot cycles by hand. It reports one line per component — captured, or
skipped and why — because a sweep that named only its successes would leave you trusting baselines
that were never taken.

  One failure does not abort it. The rest are still captured and the failures are listed at the end.

  It never FORCES the view. A steer is declined while someone is using the app (the presence gate),
  and a declined steer is reported as a skip, not an error — a background sweep must not take the
  screen. It also verifies the app actually LANDED each component before capturing: photographing
  whatever happened to be on screen under a component's name is the one mistake a baseline cannot
  make.",
    },
    CmdDoc {
        name: "frame",
        summary: "capture the mounted preview of ONE named component — you choose what to look at",
        usage: "\
USAGE:
  bsc shot frame <component> [--frames <N>] [--interval <ms>] [--out <path>] [--timeout <ms>] [--json|--pretty]

Captures the preview frame rendering <component>, wherever it sits on screen. Where `preview` shoots
whatever the Studio happens to be showing, `frame` names the SUBJECT — so a session chooses what to
photograph instead of depending on what the user last clicked.

The app resolves the rect itself from the mounted previews (the same geometry `bsc debug frames`
reports), so you never pass coordinates — and you do not need `bsc debug` in your own command surface.

  --frames <N>     a BURST of N frames (for animations) — see `bsc shot take help`.
  --interval <ms>  gap between burst frames (default 120).
  --out <path>     where to write the PNG (base for a burst). Default <shots>/<id>.png.
  --timeout <ms>   how long to wait for the app, per frame (default 8000).

NOT MOUNTED
  If no preview for <component> is mounted, this errors and NAMES what is currently showing, rather
  than silently capturing the whole screen. Land it first:
    bsc navigate component <kit> <component>

  That navigate only switches the view when the Studio is ALREADY showing (#3599). If it did not, the
